A colour palette notifies many brush tools whenever the selected colour changes. The palette keeps a list of registered listeners and calls them in a loop:

```java
public void notifyColour(Colour colour) {
    for (BrushListener listener : listeners) {
        listener.onColour(colour);
    }
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A configuration class in a painting application is written like this and is read from every tool handler:

```java
public class PaletteConfig {
    private static PaletteConfig instance;
    private PaletteConfig() { }
    public static PaletteConfig getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new PaletteConfig();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Which criticisms of this code are justified?

A report generator fixes the order of the steps and lets subclasses vary two of them:

```java
public abstract class ColourReportGenerator {
    public final Report generate() {
        Data data = load();
        Data shaped = transform(data);
        return write(render(shaped));
    }
    protected Data load() { return Data.fromDefaultSource(); }
    protected abstract Data transform(Data data);
    protected abstract String render(Data data);
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A painting tool mixes colours with interchangeable algorithms:

```java
public interface MixStrategy { void performMix(List<Colour> input); }
public class SimpleMix implements MixStrategy { ... }
public class AdvancedMix  implements MixStrategy { ... }
public class PaletteContext { private List<Colour> colours; private MixStrategy mixAlgorithm; ... }
public class PalettePolicy  { private PaletteContext context; public void configure() { ... } }
```

Using the class names from this listing: the class that holds the colours and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `SimpleMix` and `AdvancedMix` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

A reviewer rejects the following class:

```java
public class BrushService {
    private final SmtpMailer mailer = new SmtpMailer("mail.internal", 25);
    public void applyBrush(Brush brush) { /* ... */ mailer.send(brush.getUserEmail(), "..."); }
}
```

The reviewer's first objection is that `BrushService` names a concrete low‑level class instead of an abstraction such as a `Notifier` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer's fix is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two‑word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
